#
# What may be asked about, and what may be asked of it.
#
# This file is the structure: which resources exist, and which of the
# privileges make sense on each of them. It is not who may do what - that is
# an assignment, it belongs to a business rather than to the application, and
# it stays in the database, on the roles a tenant makes for itself out of the
# pairs written down here. Mixing the two into one source would mean the
# structure could change while the application is running, and the structure
# is exactly the part every request may share.
#
# NEON rather than JSON because it is the language the rest of the
# configuration is written in, and because it has comments: a permission
# nobody wrote a reason for is one nobody can decide about a year later.
#
# The keys are the values of Trilobit\Core\Security\Resource and the
# privileges are the values of Trilobit\Core\Security\Privilege. Neither list
# is repeated here - a name this file invents is refused when it is read, and
# a case missing from this file is refused too, because registration walks the
# enum and would otherwise register a resource nobody has said anything about.
#
# The resources are a tree, and the tree is in the names: a resource falls
# under its name up to the last dot, and every resource a name passes through
# has to exist, or the build does not start. There is no key for it - one
# would be a second sentence that could disagree with the name. Moving a
# resource is renaming it, which is a migration of every role holding a piece
# of it.
#
# The tree means two things and no third. Upwards it is a door: any right on a
# resource lets its holder view everything the resource falls under, so
# somebody who may work in a section may get to it. Downwards nothing is
# inherited - a pair means that pair and nothing else, so opening the
# administration is not reading every section of it. A resource something
# falls under therefore has to offer `view`.
#
# `bundle: true` lets the whole of a resource be granted, written
# `app.administration.content:*`: every privilege of it and of everything under
# it, including the ones added after the role was written. That growing is
# exactly why it is said here and not assumed - a resource that should never
# grow on its own leaves it out, and the whole of it granted on a role is then
# dropped, the way an outdated piece is dropped. The whole of any resource may
# always be taken away; a denial that grows fails in the safe direction. None
# of this is given to Nette as it is written - it is worked out while an access
# list is composed, and the list holds a plain rule of its own on every
# resource; see Trilobit\Core\Security\AccessComposition.
#
# Not every pair a role could be assembled from is checked somewhere yet, and
# that is allowed on purpose: a piece may be defined before the first section
# uses it. The other direction is the one that has to hold - a pair the code
# asks about and this file does not have is an exception at the first
# question, not a denial; see
# tests/Architecture/EveryPermissionQuestionIsPredefinedTest.
#

# The application inside one business, and what everything else falls under.
# Viewing it is the only thing asked of it, and nobody needs to be given that by
# name: any right below opens it, and no page asks for it.
#
# A bundle, because the whole of it is everything in the business, including
# sections nobody has written yet - which is what owning a business means.
# Which role may carry that is a question about the role, not about this file:
# the owner's and no other, see Trilobit\Core\Security\AccessComposition.
app:
    bundle: true
    privileges: [view]

# The administration as a whole. Opening it at all is the one thing asked of
# it, and anybody holding a right in one of its sections may open it. Its
# whole is everything in it, which is what administering a business means: a
# section added to the administration is one its administrators should have
# without anybody going back to their role.
app.administration:
    bundle: true
    privileges: [view]

# The accounts of the installation and the roles they hold. Purging is apart
# from deleting because an account is switched off rather than removed, so that
# what somebody did stays attributable - and undoing that is a different
# decision from taking the account away.
#
# No bundle: who may sign in and what they hold is the one section where a
# privilege added later must never reach somebody who was not given it by
# name. The whole administration still reaches it, because that is a decision
# about the administration and not about the accounts.
app.administration.account:
    privileges: [view, add, edit, delete, purge]

# What the public site is drawn from. Ordering is its own privilege because a
# menu is an ordering: somebody may be trusted to arrange what is there
# without being trusted to change what it says.
#
# A bundle, because a whole section of content is how an editor's role is
# meant: whatever the site learns to hold next, whoever keeps the content
# keeps that too.
app.administration.content:
    bundle: true
    privileges: [view, add, edit, delete, purge, export, change_priority]

# Where a visitor is sent. Under the application, so that the whole of the
# application reaches it; not under the administration, because it is a
# decision inside a page that answers to the public rather than a section of
# anything, so a role that may overrule it is not thereby a role that may open
# the administration. The door it opens is the application's, which no page
# asks for.
#
# No bundle: it is one decision with two privileges, and overruling where a
# visitor ends up is not something to come by without being given it.
app.redirection:
    privileges: [view, force_redirect]
